   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC040.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 41,
   "file": "SC041",
   "section": "The bill",
   "start": 302.831,
   "duration": 11.458,
   "narration": "So did losing the vultures actually cost human lives? In 2024, two economists, Eyal Frank and Anant Sudarshan, set out to measure it.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "paper",
    "title": "The Social Costs of Keystone Species Collapse",
    "sub": "Frank & Sudarshan \u00b7 American Economic Review \u00b7 2024",
    "chapter": {
     "main": "THE BILL",
     "sub": "Chapter 4"
    }
   },
   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC041.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC041.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 42,
   "file": "SC042",
   "section": "The bill",
   "start": 314.289,
   "duration": 8.667,
   "narration": "They compared districts where vultures used to be common with districts where they never were, before and after the diclofenac years.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "districts"
   },
   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC042.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC042.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 43,
   "file": "SC043",
   "section": "The bill",
   "start": 322.956,
   "duration": 7.333,
   "narration": "Their finding: in former vulture country, human death rates rose by about four point seven percent.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "bignum",
    "value": "+4.7%",
    "label": "human death rate in districts that lost their vultures",
    "tag": "ESTIMATE"
   },
   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC043.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC043.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 44,
   "file": "SC044",
   "section": "The bill",
   "start": 330.289,
   "duration": 8.625,
   "narration": "Sales of rabies vaccine went up. Water quality got worse, as more carcasses were left to rot near rivers and wells.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "icons2",
    "left": [
     "vaccine",
     "Rabies vaccine sales \u2191"
    ],
    "right": [
