   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC053.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC053.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 54,
   "file": "SC054",
   "section": "It's happening again",
   "start": 415.414,
   "duration": 13.375,
   "narration": "A 2016 study found that eight African vulture species had fallen by an average of sixty-two percent. Seven of them by eighty percent or more, in just three generations.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "africa",
    "tag": "STRONG"
   },
   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC054.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC054.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 55,
   "file": "SC055",
   "section": "It's happening again",
   "start": 428.789,
   "duration": 9.542,
   "narration": "The main killer here isn't medicine. It's poison, often laced into carcasses, and the trade in vulture parts for traditional remedies.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "plate",
    "shot": "P22",
    "move": "pull"
   },
   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC055.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC055.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 56,
   "file": "SC056",
   "section": "It's happening again",
   "start": 438.331,
   "duration": 14.958,
   "narration": "Some poachers even poison elephant carcasses on purpose, because circling vultures can lead rangers straight to an illegal kill. In one case in Namibia, around five hundred vultures died at a single carcass.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "poison",
    "tag": "STRONG"
   },
   "clip": "../09_Animation/SC056.mp4",
   "audio": "../10_Audio/narration/SC056.wav"
  },
  {
   "id": 57,
   "file": "SC057",
   "section": "It's happening again",
   "start": 453.289,
   "duration": 6.583,
   "narration": "Together, those two threats account for about ninety percent of the vulture deaths that get reported.",
   "visual": {
    "type": "card",
    "card": "bignum",
    "value": "90%",
    "label": "of reported vulture deaths in Africa: poisoning + trade in parts",
    "tag": "STRONG"
